.TH MAIL9P 4
.SH NAME
mail9p \- IMAP/SMTP mailbox as a 9P filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mail9p
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.B -t
.I seconds
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mail9p
exposes one or more IMAP4rev1 mailboxes (RFC 3501) as a Plan 9-style
filesystem so an agent can read, classify, flag, move, and (separately)
reply to mail using ordinary file operations. Credentials live in
.IR factotum (4);
.I mail9p
never sees them on its own command line.
.PP
By default it mounts at
.BR /mnt/mail .
The
.B -m
flag relocates the mount; the
.B -D
flag enables Styx tracing on stderr.
.PP
A mail server is waited for
.I seconds
(default 30) at each step: the making of a connection, and each stretch
in which nothing comes from it; one command may take twenty times that
in all.
After that, as when a server hangs up or sends more than is taken (a
line of 1MB, a literal of 64MB, 128MB in answer to one command), the
request fails with an error naming the account, and the account is
.BR disconnected :
its
.B ctl
says why, what was listed stays listed, and its next use connects
again (not sooner than ten seconds, or
.IR seconds ,
after a connection that could not be made).
Each account's requests are done one at a time by a process of its
own, so one account's server never holds up another account or the
tree.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF accounts/
.TP
.B ctl
write-only:
.BR "connect " "\fIname server\fP [\fBtls\fP|\fBstarttls\fP] [\fBsmtp=\fP\fIhost\fP]"
opens an IMAP session and best-effort SELECTs INBOX:
.B tls
(the default) speaks TLS from the start, on port 993;
.B starttls
connects in clear on port 143 and upgrades with STARTTLS, refusing a
server that declines it rather than logging in in clear.
.I Server
is a host, or
.IB host ! port
for another port (a local bridge's, say);
.BR "disconnect " \fIname\fP
removes the account's files at once and LOGOUTs.
Files open in it name nothing from then on: a later account, of that
name or another, is another account;
.BR "sync " \fIname\fP
re-fetches the folder list, re-SELECTs the current mailbox and lists
its messages again.
.TP
.B accounts/
one entry per connected account.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /ctl
read: status line
.RB ( connected | disconnected ", " "box=" "\fIname\fP, " "exists=" "N, " "unseen=" "N" );
after
.B disconnected
comes the reason in parentheses, and
.BI listed= N
follows when not every message that exists is listed (the newest
100000 are).
Write:
.BR "select " \fImailbox\fP
or
.BR sync .
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /compose
A full RFC822 message written here is sent via SMTP using
factotum credentials, to the addresses of its
.BR To: ,
.B Cc:
and
.B Bcc:
headers (every occurrence, with its continuation lines), from the
address of its
.B From:
header (the account's user if it has none).
A
.B Bcc:
header is taken out of the message before it is sent; a message that
names nobody is refused.
The message is everything written between the file's open and its
close, in as many writes as it takes (32MB at most); it is sent when
the file is closed.
A close cannot fail, so to learn what happened read the file: on the
descriptor the message was written on, the read waits for the send and
returns
.BR sent ,
or fails with the reason; opened afresh,
.B compose
gives one line about the account's last send
.RB ( "sent to"
and the recipients, or
.B "not sent:"
and the reason).
A message that was not sent is also reported on
.IR mail9p 's
standard error.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/
one entry per server-reported mailbox.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box /ctl
write:
.BR "search " \fIimap-criteria\fP
runs IMAP UID SEARCH; the matching UIDs land in the same ctl file (one
per line) and are returned on the next
.B read
of that box's ctl.
The criteria are one line: a line break in them is refused.
.BR "archive " \fIuid\fP
COPYs the message to
.B Archive
and STOREs
.BR \eDeleted .
.BR "move " "\fIuid\fP \fIdest-box\fP"
COPYs then STOREs
.BR \eDeleted ;
.I dest-box
is one of the account's boxes, as
.B boxes
names it or as the server does, and nothing else.
Operations only succeed against the currently-selected box; SELECT
via the account ctl first.
Every message is named to the server by its UID, so a message another
client has moved or removed is never mistaken for its neighbour.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid
one directory per message in the currently-selected box, named by
IMAP UID. Non-current boxes are listable but have no message
sub-entries (select the box first).
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /from
.PD 0
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /to
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /cc
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /replyto
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /subject
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /date
.PD
read-only header fields, parsed from the IMAP envelope.
.B Replyto
is the message's Reply-To, empty if it has none: where it is not
empty, that is where
.B draft-reply
sends, not to
.BR from .
A name beside an address is the sender's own text.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /flags
read: space-separated flag tokens (\fB\eSeen\fP, \fB\eFlagged\fP, ...).
.br
write: replace mode (\fB\eSeen \eFlagged\fP) sets the exact flag set;
diff mode (\fB+\eSeen -\eFlagged\fP) adds or removes individual
flags. Mixing the two styles in a single write is an error.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /body
text section of the message (everything after the first blank line).
Fetched lazily and cached.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /body.html
empty in v1. MIME parsing is out of scope; consumers that need
HTML should parse
.B raw
themselves.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /raw
full RFC822 message (headers + body). Fetched lazily and cached.
.TP
.BI accounts/ name /boxes/ box / uid /draft-reply
The text written is sent via SMTP, as
.B compose
sends (at the file's close; read back for the result), to the
message's Reply-To addresses, or its From's if it has none, with
.BR Subject ,
.BR To ,
.BR In-Reply-To ,
and
.B References
auto-injected from the original envelope (the caller's body wins if
it already supplies any of them).
The reply is from the account, and to the message's sender: a text
with a
.BR From: ,
.B Cc:
or
.B Bcc:
of its own is refused.
So is a reply to a message whose address, subject or id would add
lines of its own to the reply (a line break in one of them).
When the server has taken it, the original message is STOREd
.BR \eAnswered .
.SH CAPABILITY MODEL
The namespace is the only enforcement primitive: there is no
permission system inside
.IR mail9p .
What is bound into a name space is what its programs can do.
.PP
.B Compose
and
.B draft-reply
are the owner's: whoever can write one sends mail as the account, to
anyone
.RB ( compose )
or to a message's sender
.RB ( draft-reply ),
with nobody asked.
They are not bound into an agent's name space.
An agent that is to answer mail is given
.B /mnt/msg
instead (the message service,
.BR /docs/MESSAGE-INTEGRATION.md ):
it proposes a reply in
.BR /mnt/msg/draft ,
and the reply is sent only when the user approves the text and the
recipient shown.
.IR Nsaudit (1)
reports a grant of
.BR /mnt/mail ,
of an account's directory or of a message's as
.B DIRECT_MAIL_SEND
(severity high;
.BR /lib/veltro/nsaudit/rules/direct-mail-send ).
.PP
What may be bound for an agent is a message's read-only files
.RB ( from ,
.BR to ,
.BR subject ,
.BR date ,
.BR body ,
.BR raw ),
each by its own path.
A message's
.B flags
and a box's
.B ctl
change the mailbox (flags, search, archive, move): bind them only for
an agent that is to do that.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
IMAP credentials are read from
.IR factotum (4)
at connect time using
.PP
.B proto=pass service=imap dom=\fIhost\fP
.PP
SMTP send uses the key
.PP
.B proto=pass service=smtp dom=\fIsmtphost\fP
.PP
and, if there is none, the IMAP server's key, but only when the SMTP
host is the IMAP host or was named with
.BR smtp= .
With no
.B smtp=
the SMTP host of an IMAP server
.BI imap. rest
is taken to be
.BI smtp. rest
(of any other, the IMAP host itself); that is a guess, and a host
that was guessed is sent only a key that names it: without a
.B service=smtp
key for it the send is refused, saying so.
Sends follow the account's IMAP mode: implicit TLS on
port 465 for
.BR tls ,
STARTTLS on port 587 for
.BR starttls .
Neither is ever in clear: an SMTP server that does not offer STARTTLS,
or refuses it, is refused before the password is sent.
.B smtp=
may name
.IB host ! port
for another port.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Connect, list folders, read the latest message in INBOX, mark it
read:
.IP
.EX
mail9p
echo connect work imap.fastmail.com > /mnt/mail/ctl
ls /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes
last=`{ls /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX | grep -v ctl | tail -1}
cat /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/$last/subject
cat /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/$last/body
echo '\eSeen' > /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/$last/flags
.EE
.PP
Search and archive:
.IP
.EX
echo 'search FROM noreply@example.com' > /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/ctl
for u in `{cat /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/ctl} {
    echo 'archive '^$u > /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/ctl
}
.EE
.PP
Reply with threading headers auto-injected:
.IP
.EX
echo 'Got it, thanks.' > /mnt/mail/accounts/work/boxes/INBOX/$last/draft-reply
.EE
